{% elsif part == 'guide' %}
  <section id="shopify-section-collection-options" class="fg-actuator-guide uk-section" aria-labelledby="actuator-buying-guide">
    <div class="uk-container">
      <h2 id="actuator-buying-guide">Compare linear actuator types</h2>
      <p>Start with the motion your project needs. Use these examples to narrow your options, then check the force, stroke, speed and duty cycle for the specific variant.</p>
      <ul class="fg-actuator-guide__models">
        <li>
          <h3><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}products/light-duty-rod-actuator">Classic Rod</a></h3>
          <p>A starting point for general push-and-pull applications such as cabinet panels and adjustable furniture.</p>
          <p><strong>Compare:</strong> Force, stroke and the space needed for the rod to extend.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}products/super-duty-actuators">Super Duty</a></h3>
          <p>For projects that need more force, such as heavier hatches or machinery panels.</p>
          <p><strong>Compare:</strong> Force at the mounting point, speed under load and duty cycle.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}collections/mini-linear-actuators">Mini</a></h3>
          <p>For compact assemblies, prototypes and lightweight mechanisms with limited mounting space.</p>
          <p><strong>Compare:</strong> Retracted length, mounting dimensions and usable stroke.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}collections/track-actuators">Track</a></h3>
          <p>A moving carriage travels along the actuator body, so the assembly can fit where a projecting rod would be in the way.</p>
          <p><strong>Compare:</strong> Carriage travel, overall track length and load support.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}collections/feedback-actuators">Feedback</a></h3>
          <p>For applications where a controller needs position information or needs to coordinate multiple actuators.</p>
          <p><strong>Compare:</strong> Sensor type, controller compatibility and stroke.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}products/industrial-heavy-duty-linear-actuators">Industrial</a></h3>
          <p>For machinery and other applications with demanding force or mounting requirements.</p>
          <p><strong>Compare:</strong> Rated force, speed, mounting loads and environmental rating.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="fg-actuator-guide__steps">
        <h2>How to size an electric linear actuator</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Work out the force at the actuator.</strong> For a hinged hatch or panel, its weight alone does not tell you the required force. The pivot and mounting geometry matter.</li>
          <li><strong>Measure the stroke and both mounting lengths.</strong> Check the distance between the mounting points in the open and closed positions, plus the clearance around the mechanism.</li>
          <li><strong>Check speed and duty cycle.</strong> Decide how quickly the mechanism needs to move and how often it cycles. Compare those requirements with the selected model's ratings.</li>
          <li><strong>Match voltage, controls and environment.</strong> Confirm the rated voltage, current and controller compatibility, and check the model's rating for the conditions it will operate in.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="fg-actuator-guide__tools"><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}pages/about-linear-actuator-calculator">Calculate force and stroke</a><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}pages/wiring-diagram-generator">Find a wiring diagram</a><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}pages/contact-us">Ask FIRGELLI for sizing help</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
{% endif %}
